<article class="env-definition" id="04L0">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04L0">Definition <span data-tag="04L0">9.6.1.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We say that a morphism $f$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a <em>transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$</em> if there exists an ordinal $\alpha $ and a functor $X: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \alpha } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ with the following properties: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every nonzero limit ordinal $\lambda \leq \alpha $, the restriction $X|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \lambda }) }$ is a colimit diagram: that is, it exhibits $X( \lambda )$ as a colimit of the restriction $X|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{&lt; \lambda } )}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every ordinal $\beta &lt; \alpha $, the morphism $X(\beta ) \rightarrow X(\beta +1)$ belongs to $W$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(c)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $X(0) \rightarrow X(\alpha )$ coincides with $f$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> In this case, we say that $X$ <em>exhibits $f$ as a transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$</em>. </p>
<p>We say that $W$ is <em>closed under transfinite composition</em> if it contains every morphism which is a transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$. </p>
</article>
